  "nameStory": "The \"bolete mould\" gets its name from its sinister dining habits - it's a parasitic fungus that specifically targets bolete mushrooms as its victims. Like a fungal vampire, it completely takes over its host, earning it the alternate name \"bolete eater\" for its ability to consume and transform entire mushrooms.",
  "description": "Bolete mould is a parasitic fungus that attacks bolete mushrooms, completely transforming its victims rather than growing independently. When you find it, you're actually looking at a bolete mushroom that has been taken over and converted into something entirely different. The infected mushroom loses its original appearance and becomes covered in a whitish, golden yellow, or tan coating that represents the parasitic fungus. This fungal hijacker essentially turns healthy boletes into unrecognizable, discolored masses.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Host Transformation",
      "description": "Look for bolete mushrooms that have lost their normal appearance and are covered in a powdery, whitish to golden coating that feels dry and dusty to the touch",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Color Change",
      "description": "The infected mushroom displays unnatural whitish, golden yellow, or tan colors instead of the host bolete's original browns, reds, or other typical hues",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Texture",
      "description": "The surface feels dry and powdery rather than the typical smooth, moist texture of a healthy bolete cap",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Shape Distortion",
      "description": "The original bolete's cap and stem structure may be warped, swollen, or completely deformed from the parasitic takeover",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Location Context",
      "description": "Always found growing exactly where you'd expect to find bolete mushrooms - never growing independently on wood or other substrates",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found wherever bolete mushrooms grow in New England's forests, particularly in mixed and coniferous woods with acidic soils. Look for it in areas where boletes are common - around oak, pine, and hemlock trees where the soil is rich with organic matter. It appears at any elevation where suitable host boletes are present.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Rarely observed as most bolete activity hasn't begun yet, though late spring infections may start appearing on early-season boletes in warmer areas.",
    "summer": "Peak season for finding infected boletes as both the host mushrooms and the parasite are most active during warm, humid conditions.",
    "fall": "Continues to be common as fall bolete flushes provide fresh hosts, often persisting longer than uninfected mushrooms due to the parasitic transformation.",
    "winter": "Dried, mummified remains of infected boletes may persist under snow, appearing as tan or whitish fungal masses."
  },
  "funFact": "This fungus is such an effective body-snatcher that it can completely hijack a bolete's reproductive system, using the mushroom's own energy to produce its own spores instead of letting the bolete reproduce - it's like a sci-fi horror movie happening in miniature on the forest floor!",
  "confusionNotes": "The transformed mushrooms might be mistaken for unusual pale boletes or even puffballs, but remember that healthy boletes are never naturally whitish or powdery. If you find a \"bolete\" that looks bleached or dusty, you're likely looking at a parasitic takeover rather than a normal mushroom.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Many people don't realize they're looking at a parasitic infection when they find these pale, distorted mushrooms - they often think they've discovered an unusual white bolete species. The original bolete is essentially dead and has been converted into something completely different.",
  "compareHints": "Unlike its famous cousin the lobster mushroom (Hypomyces lactifluorum) which turns its hosts bright orange-red, bolete mould creates pale, dusty transformations in whitish to golden tones. While lobster mushroom typically attacks brittle gill fungi and becomes firm and edible, bolete mould specifically targets boletes and renders them inedible with its powdery, less dramatic transformation.",
